(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-mycobacterial-infections-vignettes"]={"title":"Mycobacterial Infections and Tuberculosis Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 44-year-old woman is in her fifth month of treatment for drug-susceptible pulmonary tuberculosis. Her symptoms have resolved and her sputum cultures have converted to negative. Which way of judging her response should be avoided?","choices":["Following sputum cultures to conversion","Reviewing adherence and adverse effects","Repeating her interferon-gamma release assay","Following her symptoms at each visit"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Skin tests and interferon-gamma release assays may stay positive after treatment, so they cannot track response. Clinical and microbiologic response, symptoms and sputum cultures followed until conversion, are what monitor therapy. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 25, 36"},{"q":"A 4-year-old girl has a tuberculin skin test as part of targeted testing. She has no symptoms and no known contact with anyone who has tuberculosis. The reading shows 12 mm of induration. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["Positive; her cut-off is 10 mm","Negative; her cut-off is 15 mm","Positive; she needs airborne isolation","Uninterpretable in a child under 5"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Children under 5 are in the 10 mm group, with increased exposure risk, selected medical risks and high-risk congregate settings. 12 mm meets 10 mm, so the test is positive; infancy and young childhood also carry a higher progression risk, so she needs evaluation for disease. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 15, 22"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man is about to start a tumor necrosis factor inhibitor. He has no symptoms of tuberculosis and has never been tested for it. Which step regarding tuberculosis is most appropriate before he starts?","choices":["Empiric latent-infection treatment","No testing because he has no symptoms","Three sputum specimens for culture","Targeted testing for tuberculosis infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tumor necrosis factor inhibitors raise the risk of progression after tuberculosis infection, and testing is targeted at people at increased risk. A skin test or interferon-gamma release assay detects the immune response to infection; a positive result then needs clinical evaluation and chest imaging. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 15, 19"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman lives in a high-risk congregate setting. She has no symptoms, no human immunodeficiency virus infection and no known recent tuberculosis contact. Her tuberculin skin test shows 11 mm of induration. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["Negative; her cut-off is 15 mm","Positive; disease is confirmed","Negative; she has no symptoms","Positive; her cut-off is 10 mm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. High-risk congregate settings are in the 10 mm group, with increased exposure risk, selected medical risks and children under 5. 11 mm meets 10 mm, so the result is positive; she then needs evaluation for disease before any preventive treatment. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 20, 22"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman who works in a setting with ongoing tuberculosis transmission has a positive interferon-gamma release assay on targeted testing. She has no symptoms, her examination is normal and her chest radiograph is normal. Which best describes her tuberculosis status?","choices":["Progressive primary tuberculosis","Reactivation (secondary) tuberculosis","Latent tuberculosis infection","Pulmonary tuberculosis disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A positive interferon-gamma release assay shows infection, and her clinical evaluation found no evidence of disease: no symptoms, a normal examination and a normal radiograph, which is usual in latent infection. Latent infection is asymptomatic and not infectious. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 10, 19, 29"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man has latent tuberculosis infection after household exposure to his brother, who has pulmonary tuberculosis; disease has been excluded in him. Besides possible drug interactions, what must be checked before a latent-infection regimen is chosen?","choices":["The source case's drug resistance","A repeat skin test in 8 weeks","Culture conversion on sputum","A blood assay to confirm the skin test"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Before a latent-infection regimen is chosen, interactions, pregnancy considerations and source-case resistance are checked, after disease has been excluded. His brother is the source case, so the brother's resistance pattern shapes which of the short regimens fits. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 37, 23"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with newly diagnosed pulmonary tuberculosis has a rapid molecular test showing rifampin resistance. Which next step is most appropriate?","choices":["Rapid susceptibility testing and expert care","Standard four-drug regimen for 6 months","Add ethambutol for 2 more months","17-week rifapentine-moxifloxacin course"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rifampin resistance requires rapid susceptibility testing and expert management. Rifampin is one of the two drugs given through the whole 6-month standard regimen, so resistance to it takes him off the standard pathway. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 34, 35, 36"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man's chest radiograph and sputum smear lead his clinician to presume pulmonary tuberculosis; culture results are weeks away. Which reporting step is appropriate now?","choices":["No report; the clinician manages it","Notify the tuberculosis program now","Report after culture confirmation","Report if drug resistance is found"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The local or state tuberculosis program is notified of presumed or confirmed tuberculosis, so reporting does not wait for culture. Public-health coordination also guides the criteria for ending isolation. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 37-year-old man's sputum molecular test confirms Mycobacterium tuberculosis. Which additional testing is part of his initial microbiologic evaluation?","choices":["A follow-up skin test","Interferon-gamma release assay","Repeat chest computed tomography","Drug-susceptibility testing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The initial evaluation obtains molecular and culture-based drug-susceptibility testing alongside smear and culture. Susceptibility results decide when ethambutol can stop and flag rifampin resistance, which needs rapid susceptibility testing and expert management. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 26, 34, 36"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman has just started standard treatment for drug-susceptible pulmonary tuberculosis. Which plan monitors her response to therapy?","choices":["A chest radiograph as the main marker","A repeat blood assay at 2 months","Sputum cultures until they convert","A repeat skin test every month"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sputum cultures are followed until conversion, with response documented at 2 months, and adherence, symptoms and adverse effects are reviewed at least monthly. Clinical and microbiologic response, not immune-response tests, monitor therapy. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 25, 27, 36"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman who grew up in an area with high tuberculosis incidence has 2 months of persistent, painless enlargement of a cervical lymph node, with fever and weight loss. Her chest radiograph is normal, and her interferon-gamma release assay is positive. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Disseminated (miliary) tuberculosis","Lymph node tuberculosis","Latent tuberculosis infection","Pulmonary tuberculosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Extrapulmonary tuberculosis can involve almost any organ, and lymph node disease presents as persistent, often painless enlargement; fever, weight loss and lymphadenopathy are clues. The positive assay shows infection, and organ-specific specimens may be needed to confirm the diagnosis. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 8, 17, 24"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man with untreated human immunodeficiency virus infection has 3 weeks of fever and weight loss with liver and spleen involvement. His chest radiograph shows diffuse tiny nodules. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Disseminated (miliary) tuberculosis","Latent tuberculosis infection","Pleural tuberculosis","Nontuberculous mycobacterial disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Disseminated tuberculosis brings fever, weight loss and multiorgan involvement, and miliary disease can produce diffuse tiny nodules on chest imaging. Massive hematogenous dissemination, including to the liver and spleen, is the risk when immunity fails, as in untreated human immunodeficiency virus infection. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 8, 12, 27, 29, 39"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man arrived 8 months ago from an area with high tuberculosis incidence. He has no human immunodeficiency virus infection, no known contact with an infectious case and no symptoms. His tuberculin skin test shows 8 mm of induration. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["Positive; any induration is positive","Negative; his cut-off is 10 mm","Positive; his cut-off is 5 mm","Positive; his origin makes it positive"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Residence in an area with high tuberculosis incidence is an exposure risk, which puts him in the 10 mm increased-exposure group, with high-risk congregate settings and children under 5. 8 mm is below 10 mm, so the result is negative for him. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 14, 22"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man starting treatment for pulmonary tuberculosis worries he will forget doses over the 6 months. Which strategy supports completing his treatment?","choices":["Taking it 2 days a week instead","Daily directly observed therapy","Self-reported pill counts","Stopping once symptoms resolve"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Daily therapy with directly observed therapy, in person or by video, supports completion of the 6-month regimen. Adherence is also reviewed at least monthly, together with symptoms and adverse effects. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 34, 36"},{"q":"A 32-year-old man who received BCG (bacille Calmette-Guérin) vaccine in infancy needs testing for tuberculosis infection before starting work in a setting with ongoing transmission. He has no symptoms. Which test is preferred?","choices":["Acid-fast sputum smear","Chest computed tomography","Interferon-gamma release assay","Tuberculin skin test"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bacille Calmette-Guérin vaccination can cause a false-positive skin test, and the blood test is preferred for most vaccinated patients because the vaccine does not cause a positive interferon-gamma release assay. It measures immune responses to tuberculosis-specific antigens and needs one visit. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 21, 24"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman has latent tuberculosis infection; disease has been excluded, she has no drug interactions and she is not pregnant. She wants the preferred short regimen with the fewest dosing days. Which regimen is given weekly?","choices":["Isoniazid for 6 months","Isoniazid plus rifapentine for 3 months","Isoniazid plus rifampin for 3 months","Rifampin for 4 months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The weekly preferred regimen is isoniazid plus rifapentine for 3 months; the other preferred short regimens, rifampin for 4 months and isoniazid plus rifampin for 3 months, are daily. Interactions, pregnancy considerations and source-case resistance are checked first. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 2-year-old boy lives with an uncle who was diagnosed with infectious pulmonary tuberculosis 6 weeks ago. The boy now has fever and cough, and his chest radiograph shows hilar adenopathy. Which stage of tuberculosis best fits?","choices":["Reactivation (secondary) tuberculosis","Contained primary infection","Progressive primary tuberculosis","Latent tuberculosis infection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Progressive primary tuberculosis is disease that develops soon after initial infection when immune containment fails. Infancy and young childhood raise progression risk, his exposure is recent, and primary disease may show hilar or mediastinal adenopathy, infiltrates or effusion. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 9, 15, 28"},{"q":"A 34-year-old nurse reads a coworker's tuberculin skin test at 60 hours and sees a wide area of redness around a smaller area of induration. Which reading practice should be avoided?","choices":["Reading it within 48 to 72 hours","Using her risk group's cut-off","Measuring the width of the erythema","Measuring the transverse induration"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The reader measures transverse induration in millimeters and does not measure erythema. Recording the redness would overstate the reaction and could turn a negative result into a false positive. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man who moved 6 months ago from an area with high tuberculosis incidence has 2 months of pleuritic chest pain, dyspnea, fever and weight loss. Imaging shows a unilateral pleural effusion with no lung infiltrate, and his interferon-gamma release assay is positive. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Spinal tuberculosis","Pleural tuberculosis","Latent tuberculosis infection","Bacterial pneumonia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tuberculosis can involve almost any organ; pleural disease presents with effusion, pleuritic pain or dyspnea, and fever and weight loss fit tuberculosis. His residence in a high-incidence area is an exposure risk and the positive assay shows infection; organ-specific specimens may be needed. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 8, 14"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has a positive interferon-gamma release assay on targeted testing. He has no symptoms. Which next step is most appropriate?","choices":["Clinical evaluation and chest imaging","Start latent-infection treatment today","Repeat the assay in 8 to 10 weeks","Airborne isolation and health reporting"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A positive interferon-gamma release assay indicates infection, but neither it nor the skin test can distinguish latent infection from disease. A positive result requires clinical evaluation and chest imaging, so disease is excluded before any latent-infection treatment. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 19, 24"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman meets the criteria for macrolide-susceptible Mycobacterium avium complex lung disease, without cavitation or severe disease. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide, ethambutol","Inhaled liposomal amikacin as first therapy","Azithromycin alone until cultures clear","Azithromycin, ethambutol and a rifamycin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Macrolide-susceptible Mycobacterium avium complex pulmonary disease is treated with azithromycin, ethambutol and a rifamycin, under specialist direction, continued for at least 12 months after culture conversion. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with cough and weight loss has a chest radiograph showing an upper-lobe infiltrate with cavitation. What does this radiograph establish?","choices":["Reactivation rather than recent infection","Suspicion that needs microbiologic testing","Confirmed active pulmonary tuberculosis","Latent tuberculosis infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Possible radiographic findings include infiltrates, cavities, nodules, adenopathy or effusion, and post-primary disease often involves the upper lobes and may cavitate. But imaging supports suspicion and cannot confirm tuberculosis or reliably determine activity; sputum microbiology is required. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 18, 27, 28, 10"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man with suspected pulmonary tuberculosis has a chest radiograph, a sputum sample sent for a rapid molecular test and a mycobacterial culture, and a tuberculin skin test placed. Which of these results is expected to take weeks?","choices":["Rapid molecular test","Tuberculin skin test reading","Chest radiograph","Mycobacterial culture"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mycobacterium tuberculosis grows slowly, so cultures can take weeks, while molecular tests can provide rapid results. That is why at least one respiratory specimen also gets a rapid molecular test, and why culture-based drug-susceptibility results arrive later. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 4, 20, 26"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman in airborne isolation for presumed infectious pulmonary tuberculosis needs transport to radiology for computed tomography. Which measure applies during transport?","choices":["Cancel imaging until cultures convert","Gloves and gown for the transport team","Source control for the patient","No precautions outside her room"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Patient source control is used during transport, while staff caring for her continue to use fit-tested N95 or higher-level respiratory protection. Her infectiousness does not end when she leaves the room; it ends by clinical, laboratory and public-health criteria. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a single sputum culture that grows Mycobacterium avium complex at a routine visit. His symptoms are at baseline and no chest imaging has been done. Which best describes his status?","choices":["Latent tuberculosis infection","Diagnostic criteria not met","Nontuberculous lung disease","Pulmonary tuberculosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A respiratory isolate alone does not prove nontuberculous disease. Diagnosis requires all domains: compatible symptoms and imaging, exclusion of alternatives and usually 2 or more sputum cultures with the same species. He has one culture, no new symptoms and no imaging. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 38, 39"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman has taken azithromycin, ethambutol and a rifamycin for macrolide-susceptible Mycobacterium avium complex lung disease for 7 months with good adherence, but her sputum cultures remain positive. Which addition may be warranted?","choices":["Inhaled liposomal amikacin","Pyrazinamide","Isoniazid","Stopping the regimen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Persistent positive cultures after at least 6 months of treatment may warrant inhaled liposomal amikacin. She has 7 months of positive cultures despite good adherence, so she meets that trigger, and therapy still runs at least 12 months after conversion. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man with human immunodeficiency virus infection shares an apartment with a roommate who has infectious pulmonary tuberculosis. He has had a cough for 8 days. Which approach should be avoided?","choices":["Waiting for 3 weeks of cough to evaluate","Evaluating him for tuberculosis disease now","Collecting three sputum specimens","Obtaining a chest radiograph"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms may be mild or absent, and a high-risk patient should not wait 3 weeks to be evaluated. Human immunodeficiency virus infection is a major progression risk and close contact is an exposure risk, so symptoms or high suspicion call for disease evaluation now. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 15, 16, 23"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman has newly diagnosed latent tuberculosis infection. Which item in her history most raises her risk of progressing to tuberculosis disease?","choices":["Prior BCG (bacille Calmette-Guérin) vaccine","Infection acquired 15 years ago","A normal chest radiograph","Current tumor necrosis factor inhibitor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tumor necrosis factor inhibitors are listed with transplantation and prolonged corticosteroid therapy among the higher risks of progression after tuberculosis infection, alongside human immunodeficiency virus infection and major immunosuppression. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 15, 21, 29"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man who travels for work needs testing for tuberculosis infection and says he cannot return to the clinic a second time within the next week. Which test fits his constraint?","choices":["Tuberculin skin test","Three early-morning sputum samples","Chest radiograph alone","Interferon-gamma release assay"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The interferon-gamma release assay needs one visit for specimen collection; it is a blood test of immune responses to tuberculosis-specific antigens. The skin test needs a return visit, because a trained reader measures it at 48 to 72 hours. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slides 20, 24"},{"q":"A 42-year-old woman is in week 4 of isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol for pulmonary tuberculosis. Susceptibility testing shows her organism is susceptible to isoniazid and rifampin. Which change is appropriate?","choices":["Stop pyrazinamide","Stop rifampin","Add moxifloxacin","Stop ethambutol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ethambutol can stop once susceptibility to isoniazid and rifampin is established. Isoniazid, rifampin and pyrazinamide continue to complete the initial 2 months, then isoniazid and rifampin continue for 4 more. — 38. Mycobacterial Infections including Tuberculosis - Bobilin - 10-29-2025 SV.pptx, Slide 34"}]};
